tle>The effects of medetomidine on maternal and fetal cardiovascular and pulmonary function, intrauterine pressure and blood flow in pregnant goats</article-title><source>Exp. Anim.</source><volume>46</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>67</fpage><lpage>73</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9027474</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Shuba1977a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shuba</surname><given-names>M.F.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The effect of sodium-free and potassium-fre solutions, ionic current inhibitors and ouabain on electrophysiological properties of smooth muscle of guinea-pig ureter</article-title><source>J. Physiol.</source><volume>264</volume><year>1977</year><fpage>837</fpage><lpage>851</lpage>a<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">845826</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Shuba1977b"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shuba</surname><given-names>M.F.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The mechanism of the excitatory action of catecholamines and histamine on the smooth muscle of guinea-pig ureter</article-title><source>J. Physiol.</source><volume>264</volume><year>1977</year><fpage>853</fpage><lpage>864</lpage>b<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">845827</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Stephensetal1977"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stephens</surname><given-names>N.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cardinal</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Simmons</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanical properties of tracheal smooth muscleeffects of temperature</article-title><source>Am. J. Physiol.</source><volume>233</volume><year>1977</year><fpage>C92</fpage><lpage>C98</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">910897</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Stulletal1997"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stull</surname><given-names>J.T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kamm</surname><given-names>K.E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Krueger</surname><given-names>J.K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>P.-J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Luby-Phelps</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhi</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ca<sup>2+</sup>/calmodulin-dependent myosin light-chain kinases</article-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Corbin</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Francis</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name></person-group><source>Signal Transduction in Health and Disease. Advances in Second Messenger and Phosphoprotein</source><year>1997</year><fpage>141</fpage><lpage>150</lpage><publisher-name>Lippincott-Raven Publishers</publisher-name><publisher-loc>Philadelphia</publisher-loc></citation></ref><ref id="SuiandKao1997a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sui</surname><given-names>J.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kao</surname><given-names>C.Y.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Properties of inward calcium current in guinea pig ureteral myocytes</article-title><source>Am. J. Physiol.</source><volume>272</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>c543</fpage><lpage>c549</lpage>a<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9124297</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="SuiandKao1997b"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sui</surname><given-names>J.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kao</surname><given-names>C.Y.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Roles of Ca<sup>2+</sup> and Na<sup>+</sup> in the inward current and action potentials of guinea pig ureteral myocytes</article-title><source>Am. J. Physiol.</source><volume>272</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>C535</fpage><lpage>C542</lpage>b<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9124296</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="VanhoutteandLorenz1970"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vanhoutte</surname><given-names>P.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lorenz</surname><given-names>R.R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effect of temperature on reactivity of saphenous, mesenteric, and femoral veins of the dog</article-title><source>Am. J. Physiol.</source><volume>218</volume><year>1970</year><fpage>1746</fpage><lpage>1750</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5446309</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Vanhoutteetal1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vanhoutte</surname><given-names>P.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Corcaud</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>de Montrion</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Venous diseasefrom pathophysiology to quality of life</article-title><source>Angiology.</source><volume>48</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>559</fpage><lpage>567</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9242153</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="WangandKotlikoff1997"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kotlikoff</surname><given-names>M.I.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Inactivation of calcium-activated chloride channels in smooth muscle by calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase</article-title><source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source><volume>94</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>14918</fpage><lpage>14923</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9405714</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Webb-peploeandShepherd1968"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Webb-peploe</surname><given-names>M.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Shepherd</surname><given-names>J.T.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Peripheral mechanisms involved in response of dog's cutaneous veins to local temperature changes</article-title><source>Circ. Res.</source><volume>23</volume><year>1968</year><fpage>701</fpage><lpage>708</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5727179</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> EC, excitation-contraction&#x0003b; MCLK, myosin light chain kinase&#x0003b; MCLP, myosin light chain phosphatase&#x0003b; STOC, spontaneous transient outward currents.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Effect of cooling (from 35 to 21&#x000b0;C) on mechanical activity of the guinea pig and rat ureter. (A and B) Effect of cooling on the amplitude of the evoked phasic contraction of the guinea pig and rat ureter, respectively. (C and D) Normalized phasic contractions recorded at 35 and 21&#x000b0;C. (E and F) Effects of cooling on the rate of force development and relaxation. The preparations were excited by electrical field stimulation with the following parameters: amplitude 4&#x02013;5 V, duration 40&#x02013;100 ms, and rate 0.025 Hz.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The effects of cooling on force kinetics. (A and B) Effect of 10&#x000b0;C cooling on the parameters of the phasic contractions (top traces) and [Ca<sup>2+</sup>]<sub>i</sub> transients (bottom traces) of guinea pig and rat ureter. (C and D) The above records superimposed and displayed on a faster time scale. The protocol of stimulation was the same as in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Fig. 1</xref>. Note the difference in the effect of cooling on the rising phase of [Ca<sup>2+</sup>]<sub>i</sub> transient and rate of rise of force.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>The effects of rapid temperature change. A jump from 22 to 32&#x000b0;C on the parameters of the phasic contraction of the rat ureter. The jump was applied during the rising phase of the force development, at a time when [Ca<sup>2+</sup>]<sub>i</sub> had almost reached its maximal value.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Temperature and the force-Ca<sup>2+</sup> relationship. (A and B) Simultaneous force and calcium records at 35 and 22&#x000b0;C in guinea pig and rat ureter. The data are presented in the form of phase-plane diagram in C and D. The arrows show the time-dependent development of the contraction, which forms a counter clockwise loop. Note the large hysteresis between the force-Ca<sup>2+</sup> relationship recorded during the rising and relaxation phase of the contraction.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Simultaneous recording of force (top trace), [Ca<sup>2+</sup>]<sub>i</sub> (middle trace), and action potential (bottom trace) taken at 35 and 22&#x000b0;C. The ureters were stimulated by suprathreshold, short duration (40&#x02013;50 ms) current pulses. The stimulation artifacts have been removed for clarity. Note the marked difference in the effect of cooling on the duration of the plateau component of the action potential between guinea pig and rat.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Cooling and the ionic currents in guinea pig ureter cells. (A and B) outward and inward currents recorded at two temperatures, during voltage steps from a holding potential of &#x02212;80 mV to &#x02212;50 to +50 mV, respectively. (C and D) Current-voltage relationships of the outward and inward currents at 35 and 22&#x000b0;C. The linear leakage current was subtracted from the overall membrane current.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Cooling and the ionic currents in rat ureter cells. (A) Inward currents recorded with cesium in the pipette, at 35 and 22&#x000b0;C, during voltage steps from &#x02212;80 to 0 mV. (B) Tail currents recorded at 35, 27, and 22&#x000b0;C at &#x02212;80 mV after depolarizing pulses to 0 mV. (C) Current voltage relationship for the early peak (Ca) current (a) and late (Ca-activated Cl) current measured at the end of the pulse (b) at 35 and 22&#x000b0;C. (D) Plot of the inward tail current amplitude following repolarization to &#x02212;80 mV versus membrane potential during the depolarizing prepulse. Linear leakage current was subtracted from the overall membrane current.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>The effect of calyculin A (1 &#x003bc;M) on force and Ca in guinea pig and rat ureter at 25&#x000b0;C. In B, three control and calyculin A records (indicated by arrowheads in A) have been enlarged to show the much more marked effects on relaxation than Ca in both species.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8465.f8"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>